1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,650
My first version played this 4K video at
just seven frames a second.

2
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:07,598
At sixty frames a second,

3
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:09,432
each frame has sixteen point seven

4
00:00:09,432 --> 00:00:11,913
milliseconds to be decoded, blurred and
shown,

5
00:00:11,913 --> 00:00:14,610
and missing that slot makes the video fall
behind.

6
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,616
On the WPF side, the trap was layout,

7
00:00:20,616 --> 00:00:25,669
because my time readout and face boxes
made WPF run a layout pass sixty times a

8
00:00:25,669 --> 00:00:29,890
second, so now the moving parts change
only pixels or a transform.

9
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:36,530
And in the app, layout passes now stay at
zero.

10
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,708
Frames are shown on a clock, and each one
has its own due time,

11
00:00:40,708 --> 00:00:45,210
so when a frame would arrive late, it is
skipped and the video stays in real time.

12
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:58,042
Decoding and blurring run on separate
threads, joined by small bounded queues,

13
00:00:58,042 --> 00:01:02,650
and when a queue is full the decoder
simply waits, which keeps memory flat.

14
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:10,546
My first mistake was dropping late frames
only at the screen,

15
00:01:10,546 --> 00:01:14,976
after they had already been decoded and
blurred, so all that work was thrown away.

16
00:01:14,976 --> 00:01:18,974
The fix was to decide earlier and skip a
frame before touching its pixels,

17
00:01:18,974 --> 00:01:22,810
and that alone took 4K60 from seven to
over thirty-two frames a second.

18
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:34,969
Running decode and blur on two threads
brought it to about fifty,

19
00:01:34,969 --> 00:01:38,745
and capping OpenCV's thread pool at four
threads took it to sixty,

20
00:01:38,745 --> 00:01:40,290
with no late frames at all.

21
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:48,261
This is the real pipeline, with three
tasks, two bounded channels,

22
00:01:48,261 --> 00:01:50,570
and a single mailbox to the UI thread.

23
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:57,530
And here it is at 4K60 with the blur on,
running at sixty frames a second.

24
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:04,527
The trade-off is that the preview can skip
a frame that would be late,

25
00:02:04,527 --> 00:02:06,050
while export never drops one.

26
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:14,250
Next, I'll show how YouTube-style timeline
previews work in WPF.

